# Citation metadata. GitHub reads this file and renders a "Cite this repository"
# button, and it is what a paper or a blog post copies instead of inventing a
# reference. It exists because measurements published here are meant to be cited and
# re-run: the boards in docs/PERFORMANCE.md carry their protocol so that someone
# else can contradict them.
#
# Keep `version` and `date-released` on the current tag. They are the two fields that
# rot, and a citation pointing at a version that never existed is worse than none.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use memra or cite its measurements, please cite it as below.
type: software
title: 'memra: a Rust + CUDA inference engine with per-device tuned defaults'
abstract: >-
  An inference engine for RTX Blackwell workstation and consumer cards, written in
  Rust and CUDA. A mechanism that wins on one supported card and loses on the other
  becomes a per-device default keyed on the device rather than a compromise applied
  to both, so an untuned command runs at that card's measured best. Speculative,
  CUDA-graphed and batched serving are each gated byte-identical to plain decode, per
  request, and the drafter proposes over a frequency-ranked 32,768-token subset while
  verification runs the target's full vocabulary.
authors:
  - given-names: Avi
    family-names: Fenesh
    alias: avifenesh
version: 0.108.0
date-released: '2026-08-23'
license: MIT
repository-code: https://github.com/avifenesh/memra
url: https://github.com/avifenesh/memra
keywords:
  - inference engine
  - speculative decoding
  - quantization
  - NVFP4
  - CUDA
  - Blackwell
  - GGUF
  - safetensors
